            <ArticleTitle>[Genetic and epigenetic factors of polycystic ovary syndrome].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1275-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1556/650.2016.30434</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The development of polycystic ovary syndrome and its exact pathophysiological mechanism is still unclear, but environmental and genetic factors likely play a role. Exposition to teratogenic effects during the prenatal development can lead to chronic diseases in the postnatal period. This finding confirms the common familial aggregation as well. A literature search was conducted up to January 1, 2016 for articles dealing with the genetic or epigenetic factors of polycystic ovary syndrome. This review will discuss the current understanding of the genetic basis and clinical presentation of this disease. Orv. Hetil., 2016, 157(32), 1275-1281.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
